<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.5.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00929</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.5.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3bf8161a-b912-449f-98f1-1223d174893a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="90d52abd-4481-4338-bd9f-d536b123fc4d" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--1a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1b355614-2928-47aa-bdb9-b75be9a3c5e5" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="It costs $40 to use 2 gigabytes of data using plan \(C\)." data-solution-options="[&quot;It costs $40 to use 2 gigabytes of data using plan \\(C\\).&quot;, &quot;It costs $40 to use 2 gigabytes of data using plan \\(D\\).&quot;, &quot;It costs $2 to use 40 gigabytes of data using plan \\(C\\).&quot;, &quot;It costs $2 to use 40 gigabytes of data using plan \\(D\\).&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The total monthly cost of a cell phone plan from Company \(C\) costs $10, plus $15 per gigabyte for data used. The total monthly cost from Company \(D\) costs $80 with unlimited data.</para>
      <para>Rule \(C\) gives the monthly cost, in dollars, of using \(g\) gigabytes of data on Company \(C\)’s plan. Rule \(D\) gives the monthly cost, in dollars, of using \(g\) gigabytes of data on Company \(D\)’s plan.</para>
      <para>Which sentence describes the meaning of the statement \(C(2)=40\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! It costs $40 to use 2 gigabytes of data using plan \(C\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="6820dc36-de9f-4726-9ff6-1a89a1be91be" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="C(4)" data-solution-options="[&quot;C(4)&quot;, &quot;D(4)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which is less, \(C(4)\)or \(D(4)\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(C(4)\) is less. \(C(4)=70\) and \(D(4)=80\). The phone plan from Company C is less expensive if 4 gigabytes of data are used.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="d5ae485c-d5e3-4d18-938b-dcfc65de291c" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="D(5)" data-solution-options="[&quot;C(5)&quot;, &quot;D(5)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which is less, \(C(5)\) or \(D(5)\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(D(5)\) is less. \(D(5)=80\) and \(C(5) =85\). The phone plan from Company D is less expensive if 5 gigabytes of data are used.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--text input-->
  <exercise data-content-id="01b2e207-fdc0-486e-a5af-d90331a0dde9" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="8">
    <problem>
      <para>For what number \(g\) is \(C(g)=130\)? </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.5.3 – 4.5.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="459a1487-e456-4175-987e-b7a08d4000af" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="4">
    <problem>
      <para>Function \(G\) takes a student’s first name for its input and gives the number of letters in the first name for its output.</para>
      <para>Find the value of \(G(\text{Jada})\). </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The name “Jada” has 4 letters.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.5. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="50204151-83a1-409c-90d5-d32808ffbaa5" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="5">
    <problem>
      <para>Find the value of \(G(\text{Diego})\).</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="de4c36d3-4c57-41cc-8605-d9865feb2be0" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Yes" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Is the following graph a function?</para>
      <figure><media alt="The figure has an absolute value function graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The \(x\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The \(y\)-axis runs from negative 4 to 8. The vertex is at the point (0, negative 1). The line goes through the points (negative 1, 0) and (1, 0)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8e377a41847030865efbafb8ae4e8c83db43dc88"/></media></figure></problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Yes, this is a function because the graph passes the vertical line test.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.5.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="d546abc7-2562-40a8-bc3c-7d3e74c54ded" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Yes" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Is the following graph a function?</para>
      <figure><media alt="The figure has a half-circle graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The \(x\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The \(y\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The curved line segment starts at the point (negative 2, 0). The line goes through the point (0, 2) and ends at the point (2, 0)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1f2d86b6714ef87d94be2f3aa4741273c2f2f4d5"/></media></figure></problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Yes, this is a function because the graph passes the vertical line test.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ff99ea32-dcd7-4674-8b3b-6b1e0ee78860" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Is the following graph a function?</para>
      <figure><media alt="The figure has two curved lines graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The \(x\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The \(y\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The curved line on the left goes through the points (negative 2, 0), (negative 4, 5), and (negative 4, negative 5). The curved line on the right goes through the points (2, 0), (4, 5), and (4, negative 5)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b27425bc88681f1b290617df73d1d63458f6116c"/></media></figure></problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! No, this is not a function because the graph does not pass the vertical line test.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.5.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
